## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a practical data structure solution for maintaining top-K items under batch updates, balancing per-update cost and output cost, and comparing against naive approaches.

## Key Concepts
- **Top-K maintenance** with dynamic count updates
- **Batch amortization** — distributing work across batch items rather than per-event
- **Heap with lazy updates** — using a min-heap for efficient top-K tracking
- **Space-time trade-offs** — comparing near-linear space with output efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
class TopKTracker {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap(); // stores (count, id) pairs
  }

  processBatch(updates) {
    // Phase 1: Apply all deltas to hashmap
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const newCount = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + delta;
      this.counts.set(id, newCount);
    }

    // Phase 2: Reconcile heap with updated counts
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const count = this.counts.get(id);
      if (this.heap.has(id)) {
        this.heap.update(id, count);
      } else if (this.heap.size < this.k) {
        this.heap.insert(id, count);
      } else if (count > this.heap.peekMin().count) {
        this.heap.replaceMin(id, count);
      }
    }
  }

  getTopK() {
    return this.heap.getAllSorted(); // returns K items sorted by count descending
  }
}
```

## Amortized Analysis
Each batch processes B events. Updating the hashmap costs O(B) total. Each heap operation costs O(log K), and at most B such operations occur per batch, yielding O(B log K) amortized time. Since B << N in practice (batch sizes are typically much smaller than total unique keys), this significantly outperforms re-scanning all N items after every batch, which costs O(N log N) per batch.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests offline reasoning for dynamic bipartiteness under batched updates, along with the ability to combine DSU with rollback and time-block decomposition.

## Key Concepts
- **Parity (Bipartiteness) DSU**: Maintains connected components with parity labels to detect odd cycles, enabling O(α(N)) union-find operations.
- **Rollback / Persistent State**: Allows reverting DSU state within a block to test multiple edge configurations efficiently.
- **Time-based Block Decomposition**: Divides updates into blocks, rebuilding the DSU at block boundaries while processing updates within blocks using rollback.
- **Trade-offs**: Balancing rebuild cost (at block boundaries) against per-update cost (within blocks).

## Impact of Batch Size B
- **Larger B**: More edges processed per batch → fewer rebuilds → lower amortized cost per edge, but higher memory overhead for rollback structures.
- **Smaller B**: More frequent rebuilds → higher amortized cost, but lower memory since fewer rollback checkpoints are needed.
- **Optimal B**: Typically Θ(√M) balances O(√M · α(N)) per batch with O(M) space.

## Code Example
```cpp
struct ParityDSU {
    vector<int> parent, rank, parity;
    struct Change { int u, oldParent, oldRank, oldParity; };
    vector<Change> stack;

    ParityDSU(int n) : parent(n), rank(n, 0), parity(n, 0) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        return find(parent[x]);
    }

    int findWithParity(int x) {
        if (parent[x] == x) return x;
        int p = findWithParity(parent[x]);
        parity[x] ^= parity[parent[x]];
        return parent[x] = p;
    }

    bool unite(int x, int y) {
        x = findWithParity(x);
        y = findWithParity(y);
        if (x == y) return parity[x] != parity[y];
        if (rank[x] < rank[y]) swap(x, y);
        stack.push_back({y, parent[y], rank[y], parity[y]});
        parent[y] = x;
        if (rank[x] == rank[y]) rank[x]++;
        parity[y] = parity[x] ^ parity[y] ^ 1;
        return true;
    }

    void snapshot() { stack.clear(); }
    void rollback() {
        while (!stack.empty()) {
            auto c = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();
            parent[c.u] = c.oldParent;
            rank[c.u] = c.oldRank;
            parity[c.u] = c.oldParity;
        }
    }
};
```

