## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic triangle counting with tight amortized analysis, batching, and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic graphs, incremental triangles
- Degree-based edge orientation, forward neighbor sets
- Amortized analysis, batch processing, space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
// high-level sketch
const Nplus = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> new Set());
const deg = new Int32Array(n);
let triangles = 0;
function addEdge(u,v){
  // decide orientation
  if (deg[u] > deg[v] || (deg[u]===deg[v] && u>v)) [u,v] = [v,u];
  // count new triangles
  let t = 0;
  for (let w of Nplus[u]) if (Nplus[v].has(w)) t++;
  triangles += t;
  Nplus[u].add(v);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
  // potentially rebalance orientation lazily
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the method adapt if deletions are allowed?
- What are practical bottlenecks on real graphs (power-law networks)?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of batch processing with dynamic statistics and tight performance guarantees, blending order-statistics trees with buffering for amortization analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Order-statistics trees or multisets with subtree sizes and prefix sums
- Bulk-merge and batch amortization techniques
- Maintaining running sums to compute L1 deviations efficiently

## Code Example
```javascript
class TwoTierMedian {
  constructor() {
    this.committed = new OrderStatisticsTree();
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  insertBatch(batch) {
    this.buffer.push(...batch);
    this.buffer.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    this.committed.bulkMerge(this.buffer);
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  getMedian() {
    return this.committed.findByRank(Math.floor(this.committed.size / 2));
  }

  getSumAbsoluteDeviations() {
    const median = this.getMedian();
    return this.committed.sumAbsoluteDeviations(median);
  }
}
```

## Bulk-Merge and Rebalancing Strategy
Sort the buffer, then merge two sorted sequences into the OST using an in-order traversal. After merging, rebalance the tree (e.g., via treap randomization or AVL-style rotations) to maintain O(log n) operations.

## Amortized Per-Element Cost
Let B be the batch size. Sorting takes O(B log B), bulk-merge takes O(B), and rebalancing adds O(B). Amortized per element: O(log B) per batch element, which is optimal compared to O(log n) per individual insert.

## Memory Bounds
- OST stores all committed elements: O(n)
- Buffer stores one batch: O(B)
- Prefix sums at each node: O(n) additional memory

## Two Optimizations
1. **Lazy Buffering**: Accumulate multiple batches before merging to reduce merge frequency
2. **Hybrid Selection**: Use quickselect on the buffer first to find median bounds before querying the OST

